Expected elements: present

Dark reads as a designed theme in its ramp — #f3f3f3/#dddddd/#a2a2a2/#919191 all land 4.68:1+ and surfaces form a real ladder (page #000, pane #1b1b1b, row #282828, badge #202020, current-line #334155) — but three colour roles are overloaded and the new fade costs measurable contrast.

Re-measuring the glyph pair independently: `⊘` (line 29) #919191 on #1b1b1b = 5.47:1; `⊙` (line 32) #06b6d4 on #334155 = 4.26:1. Normalising hue and background to alpha and binarising at 0.5, the two 9×9 glyphs share 23 of 29 lit cells — IoU 0.793, 75/81 cells agree. Unchanged. `⊙`'s ring flanks measure 1.91–3.34:1 against caps at 3.95–4.26:1, so it degrades toward two dashes — the part identical to `⊘`. The `⊙`/`·` collision is NOT real: `·` is not neutral in dark, it is the same cyan at 7.09:1, and 2×2 vs a 9×9 ring separates cleanly. Cyan carries six meanings in three tints: scrubber fill, active iteration chip, current-line rail, coverage dots, selected call-trace rail, source type tokens.

The fade is a 24px linear ramp (alpha 1.0 at y=1050 → 0.04 at y=1074). Code line 61 is the last rendered line, fully inside, nothing below it: 9.17:1 → 5.47:1 (x-height) → 3.38:1 (baseline) → 2.20:1 (descender); its coverage dot 2.43:1 vs 6.86:1 on line 60. Both light-theme figures confirmed. The RAW block's 1.22:1 surface step drops to 1.02:1 — bottom boundary erased.

Fix first: exempt the last line and marker glyphs from the mask; redesign `⊘`.

Rating: 6/10

